# Localize every user-facing string in every configured locale

# Frontend localization

Every user-facing string is a message identifier rendered through the localization library. This
includes headings, labels, buttons, placeholders, validation messages, empty/loading/error states,
notifications, accessible names, and document titles. Do not hardcode fallback prose in a component.

The configured locale set comes from `.kt-scaffold/answers.yml`; do not assume a fixed pair. Keep one
flat catalog per locale under `src/locales/<locale>.json`, with identical key sets. Use descriptive
hierarchical keys such as `<domain>.<surface>.<meaning>`, not English sentences as identifiers.

Locale bundles load lazily through one loader registry. Adding a locale requires the catalog, loader
entry, supported-locale type/value, and fallback behavior in one change. Missing translations fail
the parity gate; they do not silently fall back during development.

Use interpolation and locale-aware number, date, time, currency, and plural formatting. Do not build
a translated sentence by concatenating fragments. A localization is natural in its language, not a
word-for-word substitution, and one document uses one prose language consistently.

When locale affects API output or caching, send the resolved locale through the shared client and
include it in the cache key. Browser verification covers every affected locale or reports precisely
which locale was exercised.
